Day 68 – Introduction to Ansible and Inventory Setup

📌 What I Learned

Today I started learning Ansible, a configuration management and automation tool used to manage servers remotely.

🔹 What is Ansible?

Ansible is an automation tool that can be used to:

- Manage servers
- Install packages
- Copy files
- Manage services
- Execute commands remotely

Ansible is agentless, which means we don't need to install an Ansible agent on managed servers. It mainly uses SSH to connect to Linux servers.

🔹 Ansible Architecture

Control Node
     |
    SSH
     |
Managed Nodes

- Control Node – Machine where Ansible is installed.
- Managed Nodes – Servers managed by Ansible.
- Inventory – List of servers Ansible manages.
- Modules – Small units of work such as command, copy, yum, etc.
- Playbooks – YAML files used to define repeatable automation tasks.

🔹 What I Practiced

- Installed Ansible
- Verified Ansible installation using "ansible --version"
- Created an Ansible practice directory
- Created an "inventory.ini" file
- Checked AWS CLI and AWS region
- Started setting up EC2 infrastructure for Ansible practice
- Learned the basic concept of agentless server management

🔹 Important Commands

ansible --version

mkdir ansible-practice
cd ansible-practice

touch inventory.ini

aws configure get region

aws sts get-caller-identity

🔹 Key Takeaway

Ansible allows us to manage remote Linux servers from a central control node without installing an agent on every server.

This was my first step into configuration management and server automation.

